---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import MedicalReviewer from '../components/MedicalReviewer.astro';
import { faqPageJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const canonical = "https://www.beatingskincancer.com/questions-to-ask";
const title = "200 Smart Questions to Ask About Skin Cancer | Beating Skin Cancer";
const description = "Explore physician-created questions to ask your doctor about skin cancer diagnosis, treatment, and prevention, so you can prepare and advocate for yourself.";

// Per-section review dates. A section gets a date + <MedicalReviewer> byline + FAQ
// schema only once a board dermatologist has reviewed its on-page answers.
const melanomaDiagnosedReviewed = "2026-08-30";
const bccDiagnosedReviewed = "2026-08-31";
const melanomaLivingReviewed = "2026-08-31";
const bccLivingReviewed = "2026-08-31";
const sccDiagnosedReviewed = "2026-08-31";
const sccLivingReviewed = "2026-08-31";
const actinicKeratosisReviewed = "2026-08-31";
const preventionReviewed = "2026-08-31";

// Plain-text mirror of the answers in the "Melanoma – If You've Just Been Diagnosed"
// section, for FAQPage structured data. Keep in sync with the section's <details> copy.
const melanomaDiagnosedFaq = [
  {
    question: "What exactly is melanoma, and how is it different from other skin cancers?",
    answer: "Melanoma is a cancer of melanocytes, the cells that give skin its pigment. It's less common than basal cell and squamous cell carcinoma, but more dangerous because it can spread to lymph nodes and organs if it isn't caught early. Most melanomas show up as a new or changing mole.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why do doctors pay so much attention to how deep a melanoma is?",
    answer: "Depth predicts behavior. The deeper melanoma cells reach into the skin, the more access they have to blood vessels and lymphatic channels that can carry them elsewhere. Pathologists measure this in millimeters as the Breslow depth, and it's the strongest single factor in staging, in deciding whether you need a sentinel lymph node biopsy, and in estimating outlook.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does "stage 1 vs stage 3" mean in plain language?',
    answer: "Staging describes how far melanoma has traveled. Stage 1 means it's still confined to the skin and thin, with a high cure rate after surgery alone. Stage 3 means melanoma cells have reached nearby lymph nodes but not distant organs, so treatment usually adds immunotherapy or targeted therapy after surgery. Stage 2 sits between them.",
  },
  {
    question: "Where in the skin does melanoma start, and why can it sometimes spread?",
    answer: "Melanoma begins in melanocytes along the border between the epidermis, the outer layer of skin, and the dermis beneath it. Early on it spreads sideways and is easy to cure. If it isn't removed, it can grow downward into the dermis, where blood and lymph vessels give cells a route to lymph nodes and distant organs. That's why depth matters so much.",
  },
  {
    question: "How common is melanoma, and what are the main causes or risk factors?",
    answer: "Melanoma is the fifth most common cancer in the United States, with roughly 100,000 new invasive cases each year. The biggest risk factor is ultraviolet exposure from sun and tanning beds, especially blistering sunburns. Others include fair skin, many or unusual moles, a personal or family history of melanoma, and a weakened immune system. Sun protection lowers the risk.",
  },
  {
    question: "After a biopsy confirms melanoma, what usually happens next?",
    answer: "Your doctor reviews the pathology report, which lists the melanoma's thickness, ulceration, and other features. Most people then have a wide local excision to remove extra tissue around the biopsy site with a safe margin. Depending on thickness, a sentinel lymph node biopsy may be done at the same time. Thin, early melanomas often need nothing more.",
  },
  {
    question: "Who are the typical specialists involved — and what does each one do?",
    answer: "A dermatologist usually finds the melanoma and does the first biopsy. A surgical oncologist or dermatologic surgeon performs the wide excision and any lymph node surgery. A pathologist examines the tissue and writes the report. For advanced disease, a medical oncologist manages immunotherapy or targeted therapy, and a radiation oncologist may help.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do doctors decide whether to order more tests or imaging?",
    answer: "They base it on the melanoma's stage. For thin, early-stage melanoma with no symptoms, scans rarely help and can cause false alarms, so a skin and lymph node exam is usually enough. Thicker tumors, a positive sentinel lymph node biopsy, or symptoms like pain or weight loss prompt imaging such as CT, PET-CT, or MRI. Staging guides the choice.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does the phrase "clear margins" mean after surgery?',
    answer: 'Clear margins means the removed tissue has a border of normal skin all the way around the tumor. After surgery a pathologist checks the edges of the specimen under a microscope. If no melanoma cells touch the cut edge, margins are clear or negative and the tumor was fully removed. Positive margins mean cells reach the edge, so more surgery is usually advised. Margins appear on your pathology report.',
  },
  {
    question: "How soon does treatment usually begin, and what factors affect timing?",
    answer: "Surgery for melanoma is important but rarely an emergency. Most people have their wide excision within a few weeks of diagnosis. Timing depends on booking the right surgeon, coordinating a sentinel lymph node biopsy if one is planned, other health conditions, and sometimes arranging a second opinion. A short, planned wait to line up the right team does not worsen outcomes.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are the main treatment options for early melanoma?",
    answer: "For melanoma confined to the skin, surgery is the mainstay. A wide local excision removes the site with a margin of healthy tissue, usually in the office or as day surgery, and cures most thin melanomas. A sentinel lymph node biopsy may be added once the tumor is about 0.8 mm thick or thicker. Radiation or drug therapy is seldom needed this early.",
  },
  {
    question: "What's the difference between wide local excision and Mohs surgery for melanoma in situ?",
    answer: "Both remove the lesion with a clear margin. Wide local excision takes the visible spot plus a measured rim of normal skin in one step, and the edges are checked afterward. Mohs surgery removes tissue in thin layers, mapping and checking each layer under the microscope during the same visit, which spares healthy skin on the face. For melanoma in situ, either can be appropriate; your surgeon recommends which fits.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do doctors decide if a sentinel lymph node biopsy is needed?",
    answer: "The main trigger is tumor thickness. A sentinel lymph node biopsy is generally offered once a melanoma is around 0.8 mm deep, or thinner but ulcerated, and discussed for thicker tumors. Features like a high mitotic rate can tip the decision. It's usually skipped for very thin melanomas, where node spread is unlikely. Your age and other health conditions also count.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are the goals of immunotherapy and targeted therapy?",
    answer: "Both treat melanoma that has spread or has a high risk of coming back. Immunotherapy helps your own immune system recognize and attack melanoma cells, and can give long-lasting control. Targeted therapy uses pills that block a specific mutation, most often BRAF, to shrink tumors quickly. The aim is to lower recurrence risk after surgery or to control advanced disease.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do side effects from these treatments usually feel for patients?",
    answer: "With immunotherapy, most side effects come from an over-active immune system: fatigue, rash, itching, diarrhea, or thyroid changes. Most are mild, but some need steroids, so report new symptoms early. Targeted therapy more often brings fever, joint aches, rash, and fatigue that ease when the dose is adjusted. Many people keep working through treatment while the oncology team checks bloodwork regularly.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I manage the stress or fear that comes with a melanoma diagnosis?",
    answer: "Fear is normal, especially in the weeks before staging is finished. Many people find it helps to focus on the next concrete step, bring someone to appointments, and write questions down beforehand. Ask your team about a counselor or support group; most cancer centers offer them at no cost. Limit late-night searching, keep daily routines, and lean on people you trust.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do most people explain melanoma to their family or children?",
    answer: "Keep it simple and honest. Many people say melanoma is a serious skin cancer that the doctors caught and are treating, and that surgery removes it. With young children, short, calm explanations work best: name the illness, say who is helping, and reassure them it isn't their fault and isn't contagious. Invite questions and answer them in small doses over time.",
  },
  {
    question: "What can I expect during recovery from surgery?",
    answer: "After a wide local excision, expect a line of stitches, some bruising, and mild soreness for a week or two. You'll get wound-care instructions and usually return in one to two weeks for stitch removal and the final pathology result. Most people return to desk work within days, avoiding heavy lifting or stretching the area for a few weeks. Larger wounds may need a skin graft or flap.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are there questions I should ask about scarring and healing?",
    answer: "Yes. Helpful questions include: How long will the scar be, and which way will it run? Will you close it directly, or might it need a graft or flap? What helps it heal — scar massage, taping, sun protection? When can I exercise again? What is normal, and what signals infection? Asking before surgery lets you plan clothing and time off work.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I stay organized with appointments and test results?",
    answer: "Keep one folder or binder — paper or digital — for pathology reports, imaging, and visit summaries. Sign up for each office's patient portal and save results as they post. Keep a running list of dates, doctors, and next steps, and bring it to every visit. Jot down questions as they occur to you. A single point person, such as a spouse, can help track everything.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are smart questions to ask at my first oncology or surgery visit?",
    answer: "Good openers: What is my exact diagnosis and stage? How thick was the melanoma, and was it ulcerated? What surgery do you recommend, and will I need a sentinel lymph node biopsy? Do I need any scans? What are the next steps and the expected timeline? Who do I call with problems? Ask for the plan in writing and bring someone to take notes.",
  },
  {
    question: "What information should I bring to help my doctor make the best plan?",
    answer: "Bring your biopsy pathology report, plus the slides or tissue block if you were treated elsewhere, and any earlier skin-cancer history or photos of the spot. List your medications, allergies, and past surgeries, and note any family history of melanoma. Add insurance details and a written list of questions. The actual pathology material lets a new team confirm the diagnosis without repeating the biopsy.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I make sure I understand my pathology report when it's explained?",
    answer: 'Ask for a copy ahead of time and read it alongside our pathology report guide. In the visit, ask the doctor to translate each line — Breslow thickness, ulceration, margins, mitotic rate, and stage. Say it back in your own words, then ask, "What does this mean for my treatment and outlook?" Request a written summary, and feel free to ask again next time.',
  },
  {
    question: "What are the pros and cons of getting a second opinion at a melanoma center?",
    answer: "Pros: high-volume centers re-read your pathology, confirm the stage, offer the newest surgical and drug options, and can open access to clinical trials, and many patients simply feel more confident. Cons: added travel, appointments, and cost, a short delay, and sometimes conflicting advice to sort out. Most melanoma specialists expect and welcome second opinions, and a brief planned delay is safe.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I take notes or record my visit so I don't miss important details?",
    answer: "Bring a notebook or use your phone, and record the date, the main points, and any numbers such as thickness or stage. Bring a second person to listen and write while you focus on the conversation. Ask permission to make an audio recording; most doctors agree. Afterward, read the visit summary in the patient portal and send follow-up questions there. Keep it all in one place.",
  },
];

// Plain-text mirror of the answers in the "Basal Cell Carcinoma – If You've Just
// Been Diagnosed" section, for FAQPage structured data. Keep in sync with the
// section's <details> copy.
const bccDiagnosedFaq = [
  {
    question: "What exactly is basal cell carcinoma, and how is it different from other types of skin cancer?",
    answer: "Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer. It starts in the basal cells at the bottom of the outer skin layer, usually on sun-exposed areas like the face, ears, and neck. Unlike melanoma, it very rarely spreads to other organs, and it grows more slowly than squamous cell carcinoma. Left untreated, though, it can invade nearby tissue.",
  },
  {
    question: "Why do doctors say it grows slowly — does that mean I shouldn't worry?",
    answer: "Slow growth means you usually have time to plan treatment rather than rush, and it's a big reason basal cell carcinoma is so rarely life-threatening. It doesn't mean you can ignore it. Over months to years an untreated tumor can enlarge, ulcerate, and destroy nearby skin, cartilage, or bone, making later removal harder and more disfiguring. Prompt treatment while it's small keeps the procedure simple.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does it mean when they say it "almost never spreads"?',
    answer: "It means metastasis — cancer cells breaking away and traveling through blood or lymph to lymph nodes or organs — happens in far fewer than 1 in 1,000 basal cell carcinomas. Almost all stay put as a local growth on the skin. That's why staging scans and lymph node checks aren't routine for BCC, unlike melanoma. The main risk is local damage if it's left alone.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are there different kinds of basal cell carcinoma, and how do they behave?",
    answer: "Yes. The common subtypes are nodular (a pearly bump, often on the face), superficial (a flat pink patch, more common on the trunk), and infiltrative or morpheaform (scar-like, with roots that extend beyond what the eye sees). Nodular and superficial forms are usually straightforward to treat. Infiltrative and morpheaform types are more aggressive locally and often need Mohs surgery.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do doctors confirm that a spot is a basal cell — is biopsy always needed?",
    answer: "A dermatologist may strongly suspect BCC from its look and from dermoscopy (a lighted magnifier), but a skin biopsy is what confirms it. A small sample is numbed and removed, then a pathologist examines it under the microscope to identify the cancer and its subtype. That result guides which treatment fits. Biopsy is nearly always done before treatment.",
  },
  {
    question: "After being told I have basal cell carcinoma, what are the typical next steps?",
    answer: "Usually your dermatologist reviews the biopsy result with you, explains the subtype and location, and recommends a treatment — most often a minor in-office procedure scheduled within a few weeks. There's rarely a need for scans or blood tests. You'll get wound-care instructions afterward and a follow-up skin exam. Because a first BCC raises the odds of another, regular full-skin checks become part of your routine.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do doctors decide whether surgery, scraping, freezing, or cream treatment is best?",
    answer: "They weigh the subtype, size, and location, whether it's a first tumor or a recurrence, and your overall health and preferences. Aggressive subtypes or spots on the face usually get surgery, often Mohs, for the highest cure rate. Small, superficial tumors on the trunk may suit scraping and burning, cryotherapy, or a prescription cream. Cure rates and trade-offs differ.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What does it mean when they talk about "low-risk" or "high-risk" basal cell cancers?',
    answer: 'These labels predict how likely a tumor is to come back after treatment, not whether it will spread. High-risk features include an aggressive subtype under the microscope, larger size, location on the "mask" area of the face, ears, hands, or feet, poorly defined borders, a recurrent tumor, or a weakened immune system. High-risk BCCs usually get Mohs surgery for the best chance of a lasting cure.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between "clear margins" and "positive margins" on a report?',
    answer: 'After a tumor is removed, a pathologist checks the edges of the sample. "Clear" (negative) margins mean normal tissue surrounds the tumor, so it was likely all taken out. "Positive" margins mean cancer cells reach the cut edge, so some may remain and more treatment — another excision, Mohs, or occasionally radiation — is usually recommended.',
  },
  {
    question: "Why do doctors sometimes recommend Mohs surgery instead of a regular excision?",
    answer: "Mohs surgery removes the tumor layer by layer, checking 100% of each layer's edges under a microscope during the same visit before closing the wound. That gives the highest cure rate while sparing the most healthy tissue — valuable on the face, where a standard excision samples only part of the margin and removes a wider safety border. It's favored for high-risk, recurrent, or cosmetically sensitive BCCs.",
  },
  {
    question: "What happens during Mohs surgery, and how long does it usually take?",
    answer: "It's done awake under local anesthesia in the office. The surgeon removes the visible tumor plus a thin margin, then you wait — often 30 to 60 minutes — while the tissue is mapped and read under the microscope. If any edge still shows cancer, only that area is re-excised, and the cycle repeats. Most cases finish in one to three rounds over a morning, ending with wound repair.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are the pros and cons of topical treatments like imiquimod or 5-FU?",
    answer: "Creams such as imiquimod or 5-fluorouracil are applied at home for several weeks and leave no surgical scar, which appeals for small, superficial BCCs on the trunk or limbs. The trade-offs: cure rates are lower than surgery, the skin gets red, crusted, and sore during treatment, and margins can't be checked, so follow-up matters. They aren't used for nodular or facial tumors.",
  },
  {
    question: "How effective is freezing (cryotherapy) or scraping and burning (ED&C)?",
    answer: "For carefully chosen low-risk BCCs — small, superficial, away from the face — electrodesiccation and curettage (scraping then burning the base) cures roughly 90 to 95 percent, and cryotherapy (freezing with liquid nitrogen) is similar in expert hands. Both are quick and inexpensive but leave a pale, round scar and don't allow margin checking. They're avoided for aggressive subtypes or high-risk sites, where surgery is safer.",
  },
  {
    question: "When would radiation be used for basal cell carcinoma?",
    answer: "Radiation is an option when surgery isn't a good fit — for example, a frail or elderly patient, a tumor in a spot that's hard to reconstruct, or a patient who declines an operation. It's also given after surgery when margins stay positive or nerves are involved. It avoids cutting but needs many visits over weeks, can't check margins, and may age the treated skin.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What are "hedgehog inhibitors," and who might need those medicines?',
    answer: "Hedgehog inhibitors (vismodegib and sonidegib) are daily pills that block a signaling pathway most BCCs use to grow. They're reserved for the rare advanced cases — a tumor too large or deep for surgery and radiation, or one that has spread. They can shrink these tumors, but side effects like muscle cramps, taste loss, and hair thinning are common. Most people with BCC never need them.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I stay calm while waiting for my procedure?",
    answer: "It helps to keep the odds in view: basal cell carcinoma is almost always cured by a single outpatient procedure and rarely threatens health. Write down your questions, bring someone to the appointment, and ask the office to walk you through the day step by step. Basic stress tools — sleep, movement, limiting late-night searching — matter too.",
  },
  {
    question: "What does recovery usually look like for small vs. large basal cell removals?",
    answer: "A small removal closed with a few stitches typically means mild soreness, a bandage for a day or two, stitches out in one to two weeks, and a thin line that fades over months. Larger removals — or repairs using a skin flap or graft — bring more swelling, bruising, activity limits, and a longer-healing scar. Your team gives site-specific wound care.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I talk to my doctor about scarring or cosmetic concerns?",
    answer: "Raise it before the procedure, not after. Ask where the scar will sit, how long it will be, which repair options exist, and whether Mohs would spare more tissue. Ask about scar-care steps — taping, silicone gel, sun protection, and when laser or revision could help if you're unhappy later. A good surgeon expects these questions and will plan the closure with your appearance in mind.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is it okay to ask about second opinions or treatment alternatives?",
    answer: "Yes — it's routine and expected, and BCC's slow growth means a short delay to get one is rarely a problem. A second dermatologist or a Mohs surgeon can confirm the biopsy reading and review whether surgery, radiation, or a topical option best fits your tumor, your other health conditions, and your priorities. Bring your pathology report and photos so the discussion is concrete.",
  },
  {
    question: "What does follow-up care look like after treatment — is it lifelong?",
    answer: "Yes, ongoing. After a BCC, about 4 in 10 people develop another skin cancer within five years, so dermatologists usually schedule full-skin exams every 6 to 12 months, sometimes more often at first. Visits include checking the treated site for recurrence and scanning the rest of your skin for new spots. Monthly self-checks fill the gaps between visits.",
  },
  {
    question: "What questions should I ask before agreeing to a specific treatment?",
    answer: "Useful ones: What subtype and risk level is my BCC? Why this treatment over the others? What's its cure rate for a tumor like mine? What will the scar look like, and who does the repair? What are the risks and the recovery time? What happens if margins aren't clear? And how will we follow up?",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I make sure I understand what's written in my pathology report?",
    answer: "Ask for a copy and go through it with your dermatologist. Key lines are the diagnosis and subtype (nodular, superficial, infiltrative, morpheaform), the margin status (clear or involved), tumor size, and any mention of nerve or deep involvement. Ask the doctor to translate each phrase and say what it changes about your plan.",
  },
  {
    question: "What should I expect in the days right after surgery?",
    answer: "Expect some soreness, mild swelling, and possibly a bruise around the site. You'll keep the wound covered, often apply plain petroleum jelly, and avoid heavy lifting or strenuous exercise for a week or two so the repair isn't strained. A little oozing early on is normal. Take acetaminophen if your surgeon okays it. Written instructions cover bathing and dressing changes.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I know if my wound is healing normally?",
    answer: "Normal healing looks like gradually decreasing soreness, edges staying together, and a scab or pink new tissue forming. Warning signs to call about: spreading redness, warmth, increasing pain after day two or three, thick yellow-green pus, a bad smell, fever, or bleeding that won't stop with gentle pressure. Reopening of the wound also warrants a call. When in doubt, phone the office — they would rather hear from you.",
  },
  {
    question: "What should I ask about protecting the treated area from the sun?",
    answer: "Fresh scars sunburn easily and can darken permanently, so ask how long to keep the site fully shaded or taped, when you can apply sunscreen to it, and which SPF and formula suit healing skin. Also ask about protecting the rest of your skin, since sun exposure drove this cancer and raises the odds of the next.",
  },
];

// Plain-text mirror of the answers in the "melanoma-living" section, for FAQPage
// structured data. Keep in sync with the section's <details> copy.
const melanomaLivingFaq = [
  {
    question: "What is a typical follow-up schedule after melanoma treatment?",
    answer: "Follow-up depends on stage. After an early, thin melanoma, many people see a dermatologist every 6–12 months for a full skin check. Higher-stage melanoma often means visits every 3–6 months for the first few years, sometimes with imaging, then spacing out over time. Most doctors continue skin exams for life. Our guide on what to expect after diagnosis covers the rhythm.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does the risk of recurrence change over time?",
    answer: "Recurrence risk is highest in the first two to three years after treatment, which is why check-ups are most frequent then. The risk gradually falls the longer you stay clear, though it never reaches zero, and late recurrences can occur years later, especially with thicker melanomas. Thinner, earlier-stage melanomas carry a much lower risk overall. Your Breslow depth and stage shape your personal numbers.",
  },
  {
    question: "What kinds of checkups or imaging are done for different stages?",
    answer: "For early-stage melanoma, follow-up is usually just a physical skin and lymph-node exam, no routine scans. For stage 2B and higher, doctors may add imaging such as CT, PET-CT, or brain MRI every 6–12 months for a few years to catch spread early. Blood tests aren't reliable for tracking melanoma. Our stage-meaning guide explains how stage drives these choices.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are common signs of melanoma coming back in the skin or lymph nodes?",
    answer: "Watch for a new lump or firm nodule under or near the surgical scar, a change in scar color, or a new pigmented spot beside it. Swollen, firm lymph nodes in the neck, armpit, or groin that don't settle are another sign. Fatigue, weight loss, or a lasting cough or headache matter too. Our melanoma overview explains how it spreads.",
  },
  {
    question: "What's the difference between a new melanoma and a recurrence of the old one?",
    answer: "A recurrence is the original melanoma regrowing, near the scar, in nearby lymph nodes, or at a distant site, and it carries the same stage history. A new primary melanoma is a completely separate cancer that starts fresh somewhere else on the skin. People who've had one melanoma have a higher chance of a second, which is why long-term follow-up stays lifelong.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I perform a useful self-skin exam each month?",
    answer: "Once a month, in good light and using a full-length and a hand mirror, check every area, including your scalp, ears, back, buttocks, soles, and between toes. Look for anything new, changing, or different from your other spots, and feel your scar and nearby lymph nodes for lumps. A partner can check hard-to-see places. Our guide to warning signs of change lists what to flag.",
  },
  {
    question: "What should I look for around my surgical scar or lymph-node area?",
    answer: "Run your fingertips over and around the scar every month. You're feeling for a new bump, thickening, or firm nodule in or under the skin, and looking for new pigment, redness, or a sore that won't heal. In the nearby lymph-node basin, feel for swollen or hard nodes. Most changes are harmless scar tissue, but anything new that grows or persists over a few weeks deserves a call.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are mole-mapping apps or total-body photos worth using?",
    answer: "Professional total-body photography, taken in a clinic and reviewed by your dermatologist, is genuinely useful for anyone with many moles or a melanoma history; it gives a baseline to compare against. Smartphone mole-mapping apps can help you organize your own photos, but consumer \"AI\" risk scores aren't accurate enough to rule cancer in or out. Use them as a tracking tool, not a diagnosis — see tracking changes over time.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I tell when a mole looks \"different enough\" to get checked?",
    answer: "Two rules help. The ABCDEs: Asymmetry, an irregular Border, uneven Color, Diameter over about 6 mm, and any Evolving change in size, shape, or color. The \"ugly duckling\" sign: a spot that stands out because it doesn't match your other moles. Change over weeks to months matters more than a single feature. When in doubt, have it looked at. Our guide on what makes a mole atypical goes deeper.",
  },
  {
    question: "When should I call the doctor versus wait for my next visit?",
    answer: "Call promptly for a new lump near your scar or in a lymph-node area, a mole that changes quickly, a spot that bleeds or won't heal, or new symptoms like a persistent cough, headache, bone pain, or unexplained weight loss. Routine things, one slightly dry patch or a stable freckle, can wait for your scheduled visit. When unsure, a quick call is always reasonable.",
  },
  {
    question: "What daily habits lower the chance of getting another melanoma?",
    answer: "The biggest levers are sun habits: seek shade at midday, wear a broad-brimmed hat and UPF clothing, apply SPF 30+ to exposed skin daily, and never use tanning beds. Do a monthly self-exam, keep every dermatology appointment, don't smoke, and treat any suspicious spot early. None of this guarantees prevention, but together it meaningfully cuts risk. Our prevention guide has the details.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do SPF, shade, and clothing work together to protect me?",
    answer: "Think in layers. Shade and tightly woven or UPF-rated clothing block most UV before it reaches skin and don't wear off. Sunscreen covers what clothing can't, the face, ears, neck, and hands, but only if you use enough and reapply every two hours. A wide-brimmed hat and UV-blocking sunglasses finish the job. No single method is complete; combined, they add up.",
  },
  {
    question: "Is it safe to get some sun for vitamin D if I've had melanoma?",
    answer: "Most dermatologists advise getting vitamin D from food and supplements rather than deliberate sun exposure, especially after a melanoma. The amount of UV that raises vitamin D also damages skin, and there's no \"safe\" tanning dose for someone at higher risk. A daily vitamin D supplement, with a blood test to guide the dose, achieves the same goal without the UV. Discuss the dose with your doctor.",
  },
  {
    question: "What is nicotinamide, and how does it help prevent future skin cancers?",
    answer: "Nicotinamide is a form of vitamin B3, sold over the counter as a supplement (not the same as niacin, which causes flushing). In studies of people with a history of non-melanoma skin cancers, 500 mg twice daily reduced new basal and squamous cell cancers by about a fifth. Evidence for melanoma prevention is weaker. Many dermatologists suggest it for high-risk patients; ask whether it fits your situation.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do medications or immune conditions change my skin-cancer risk?",
    answer: "A weakened immune system, from organ-transplant drugs, long-term immune-suppressing medicines, HIV, or blood cancers like leukemia, raises the risk of new skin cancers and can make them behave more aggressively. Some blood-pressure and antibiotic drugs increase sun sensitivity. Tell your dermatologist about every medication and condition; it changes how often you should be screened and how quickly spots are treated.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do people cope with the fear of recurrence over the years?",
    answer: "Fear of recurrence is one of the most common lasting effects of a cancer diagnosis, and it tends to spike before check-ups and scans. People manage it by keeping to a clear follow-up plan so vigilance has boundaries, learning which symptoms actually warrant a call, staying connected to others who understand, and using counseling or structured techniques when the worry disrupts daily life. It usually eases with time.",
  },
  {
    question: "What are healthy ways to balance vigilance with peace of mind?",
    answer: "A workable balance is doing the few things that matter and then letting go: one monthly self-exam on a set date, keeping scheduled dermatology visits, and protecting your skin from the sun daily. Outside those, try not to re-check spots every day, which tends to raise anxiety without adding safety. Write down real changes to discuss at your next visit rather than researching them at midnight.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are there support groups or online communities for melanoma survivors?",
