          <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm p-8">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-navy mb-4">Melanoma – If You've Just Been Diagnosed</h2>
            <p class="text-slate">Starter questions to help you understand your diagnosis and what comes next.</p>
            <MedicalReviewer date={melanomaDiagnosedReviewed} tone="light" class="mb-6" />
            <ol class="space-y-4 text-slate leading-relaxed">
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>What exactly is melanoma, and how is it different from other skin cancers?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Melanoma is a cancer of melanocytes, the cells that give skin its pigment. It's less common than <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">basal cell</a> and <a href="/squamous-cell-carcinoma" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">squamous cell carcinoma</a>, but more dangerous because it can spread to lymph nodes and organs if it isn't caught early. Most melanomas show up as a new or changing mole. Our <a href="/melanoma" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">melanoma overview</a> explains the basics.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=What%20exactly%20is%20melanoma%2C%20and%20how%20is%20it%20different%20from%20other%20skin%20cancers%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>Why do doctors pay so much attention to how deep a melanoma is?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Depth predicts behavior. The deeper melanoma cells reach into the skin, the more access they have to blood vessels and lymphatic channels that can carry them elsewhere. Pathologists measure this in millimeters as the <a href="/melanoma/breslow-depth-explained" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">Breslow depth</a>, and it's the strongest single factor in staging, in deciding whether you need a <a href="/melanoma/sentinel-lymph-node-biopsy" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">sentinel lymph node biopsy</a>, and in estimating outlook.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=Why%20do%20doctors%20pay%20so%20much%20attention%20to%20how%20deep%20a%20melanoma%20is%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>What does "stage 1 vs stage 3" mean in plain language?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Staging describes how far melanoma has traveled. Stage 1 means it's still confined to the skin and thin, with a high cure rate after surgery alone. Stage 3 means melanoma cells have reached nearby lymph nodes but not distant organs, so treatment usually adds immunotherapy or targeted therapy after surgery. Stage 2 sits between them. Our <a href="/melanoma/melanoma-stage-meaning" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">stage-meaning guide</a> walks through each one.</p>
