                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Melanoma is the fifth most common cancer in the United States, with roughly 100,000 new invasive cases each year. The biggest risk factor is ultraviolet exposure from sun and tanning beds, especially blistering sunburns. Others include fair skin, many or <a href="/atypical-nevi" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">unusual moles</a>, a personal or family history of melanoma, and a weakened immune system. <a href="/prevention" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">Sun protection</a> lowers the risk.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=How%20common%20is%20melanoma%2C%20and%20what%20are%20the%20main%20causes%20or%20risk%20factors%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>After a biopsy confirms melanoma, what usually happens next?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Your doctor reviews the <a href="/melanoma/pathology-report" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">pathology report</a>, which lists the melanoma's thickness, ulceration, and other features. Most people then have a wide local excision to remove extra tissue around the biopsy site with a safe margin. Depending on thickness, a sentinel lymph node biopsy may be done at the same time. Thin, early melanomas often need nothing more. See <a href="/melanoma/what-to-expect-after-diagnosis" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">what to expect after diagnosis</a>.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=After%20a%20biopsy%20confirms%20melanoma%2C%20what%20usually%20happens%20next%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>Who are the typical specialists involved — and what does each one do?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>A dermatologist usually finds the melanoma and does the first biopsy. A surgical oncologist or dermatologic surgeon performs the wide excision and any lymph node surgery. A pathologist examines the tissue and writes the report. For advanced disease, a medical oncologist manages immunotherapy or targeted therapy, and a radiation oncologist may help. Learn <a href="/melanoma/finding-a-melanoma-specialist" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">how to find a melanoma specialist</a>.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=Who%20are%20the%20typical%20specialists%20involved%20%E2%80%94%20and%20what%20does%20each%20one%20do%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>How do doctors decide whether to order more tests or imaging?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>They base it on the melanoma's stage. For thin, early-stage melanoma with no symptoms, scans rarely help and can cause false alarms, so a skin and lymph node exam is usually enough. Thicker tumors, a positive <a href="/melanoma/sentinel-lymph-node-biopsy" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">sentinel lymph node biopsy</a>, or symptoms like pain or weight loss prompt imaging such as CT, PET-CT, or MRI. <a href="/melanoma/melanoma-stage-meaning" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">Staging</a> guides the choice.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=How%20do%20doctors%20decide%20whether%20to%20order%20more%20tests%20or%20imaging%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
                  <summary class="flex items-start gap-2 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden font-medium text-navy hover:text-teal transition-colors">
                    <Icon name="lucide:chevron-right" size={18} class="mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-teal transition-transform group-open:rotate-90" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <span>What does the phrase "clear margins" mean after surgery?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>"Clear margins" means the removed tissue has a border of normal skin all the way around the tumor. After surgery a pathologist checks the edges of the specimen under a microscope. If no melanoma cells touch the cut edge, margins are "clear" or "negative" and the tumor was fully removed. "Positive" margins mean cells reach the edge, so more surgery is usually advised. Margins appear on your <a href="/melanoma/pathology-report" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">pathology report</a>.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm">
                      <a
                        href="https://chatgpt.com/g/g-69045e9a6ad8819192dbd404ede6db6d-beating-skin-cancer-navigator?prompt=What%20does%20the%20phrase%20%22clear%20margins%22%20mean%20after%20surgery%3F"
                        target="_blank"
                        rel="noopener noreferrer"
                        class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors"
                      >
                        Still need more clarity? Chat with the Navigator <Icon name="lucide:arrow-right" size={14} aria-hidden="true" />
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </details>
              </li>
              <li>
                <details class="group border-b border-slate/10 pb-4">
