                    <span>What exactly is basal cell carcinoma, and how is it different from other types of skin cancer?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Basal cell carcinoma is the most common skin cancer. It starts in the basal cells at the bottom of the outer skin layer, usually on sun-exposed areas like the face, ears, and neck. Unlike melanoma, it very rarely spreads to other organs, and it grows more slowly than <a href="/squamous-cell-carcinoma" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">squamous cell carcinoma</a>. Left untreated, though, it can invade nearby tissue. Our <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">basal cell carcinoma overview</a> covers the basics.</p>
