                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Slow growth means you usually have time to plan treatment rather than rush, and it's a big reason basal cell carcinoma is so rarely life-threatening. It doesn't mean you can ignore it. Over months to years an untreated tumor can enlarge, ulcerate, and destroy nearby skin, cartilage, or bone, making later removal harder and more disfiguring. Prompt treatment while it's small keeps the procedure simple. See <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma/spread-and-seriousness" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">how BCC spreads and why it's still serious</a>.</p>
