                    <p>Yes. The common subtypes are nodular (a pearly bump, often on the face), superficial (a flat pink patch, more common on the trunk), and infiltrative or morpheaform (scar-like, with roots that extend beyond what the eye sees). Nodular and superficial forms are usually straightforward to treat. Infiltrative and morpheaform types are more aggressive locally and often need Mohs surgery. Our <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma/types" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">guide to BCC types</a> describes each one.</p>
