                    <span>What is the difference between "clear margins" and "positive margins" on a report?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>After a tumor is removed, a pathologist checks the edges of the sample. "Clear" (negative) margins mean normal tissue surrounds the tumor, so it was likely all taken out. "Positive" margins mean cancer cells reach the cut edge, so some may remain and more treatment — another excision, Mohs, or occasionally radiation — is usually recommended. Our <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma/biopsy-meaning" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">biopsy-meaning</a> page explains report language.</p>
