                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>It's done awake under local anesthesia in the office. The surgeon removes the visible tumor plus a thin margin, then you wait — often 30 to 60 minutes — while the tissue is mapped and read under the microscope. If any edge still shows cancer, only that area is re-excised, and the cycle repeats. Most cases finish in one to three rounds over a morning, ending with wound repair. Our <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma/recovery-after-surgery" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">recovery-after-surgery</a> page covers what follows.</p>
