                    <p>Radiation is an option when surgery isn't a good fit — for example, a frail or elderly patient, a tumor in a spot that's hard to reconstruct, or a patient who declines an operation. It's also given after surgery when margins stay positive or nerves are involved. It avoids cutting but needs many visits over weeks, can't check margins, and may age the treated skin. Our <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma/treatment-options" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">treatment options</a> page lists when it's chosen.</p>
