                    <p>Hedgehog inhibitors (vismodegib and sonidegib) are daily pills that block a signaling pathway most BCCs use to grow. They're reserved for the rare advanced cases — a tumor too large or deep for surgery and radiation, or one that has spread. They can shrink these tumors, but side effects like muscle cramps, taste loss, and hair thinning are common. Most people with BCC never need them. See <a href="/basal-cell-carcinoma/spread-and-seriousness" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">spread and seriousness</a>.</p>
