                    <p>Standard excision is a one-time surgery: the doctor numbs the area, cuts out the tumor with a measured margin, and closes it, with pathology confirming clear edges days later. Radiation therapy uses focused X-ray beams over several sessions across weeks, with no cutting. Surgery is faster, gives immediate margin information, and is usually first choice. Radiation suits people who can't have surgery or need extra treatment for high-risk features. Our <a href="/squamous-cell-carcinoma/treatment-options" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">treatment options page</a> compares them.</p>
