                    <span>What is an actinic keratosis, and why do doctors call it a "precancer"?</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>An actinic keratosis (AK) is a rough, scaly patch on skin damaged by years of sun or tanning beds. Doctors call it a "precancer" because some of its cells carry early changes that can, over time, progress into squamous cell carcinoma. Most AKs never become cancer, but there's no reliable way to tell which will, so they're watched or treated. See our <a href="/actinic-keratosis/what-is-ak-vs-skin-cancer" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">AK vs. skin cancer guide</a>.</p>
