                  </summary>
                  <div class="mt-3 ml-7 space-y-3">
                    <p>Ultraviolet light damages the DNA inside skin cells called keratinocytes. Most damage is repaired, but decades of exposure let mutations build up in a patch of skin. Those cells start growing abnormally, producing the dry, scaly surface of an AK. The surrounding area, often called "sun-damaged" or "field" skin, carries similar hidden changes even where it looks normal. This is why AKs tend to appear in clusters on the face, scalp, ears, and hands.</p>
