                    <p>A typical AK is a small patch, often less than a quarter-inch, with a rough, dry, or sandpapery texture — sometimes easier to feel than to see. Colors range from skin-toned to pink, red, or brownish, and the surface may be scaly or crusty. Some feel tender, itchy, or prickly. They favor sun-exposed areas. A thick, hard, or rapidly growing bump should be shown to a dermatologist. Compare features in our <a href="/actinic-keratosis" class="text-teal hover:text-teal/80 hover:underline transition-colors">actinic keratosis overview</a>.</p>
